---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro'
import { DebtChart } from '../../components/islands/DebtChart'
import { StateGiveGet } from '../../components/islands/StateGiveGet'
import { StateTaxMix } from '../../components/islands/StateTaxMix'
import { debt, statesBalance, statesTaxMix, vintageOf } from '../../data'

// Every Government sibling section adds its own id to this Set once built;
// the union of every branch's ids is what belongs here after all of them
// merge (see the plan's cross-PR conflict register for #14).
const built = new Set(['forty-trillion', 'by-state'])

const sections = [
  { id: 'forty-trillion', label: 'The $40 trillion' },
  { id: 'who-holds-it', label: 'Who holds it' },
  { id: 'how-old', label: 'How old is the debt' },
  { id: 'whole-budget', label: 'The whole budget' },
  { id: 'structural-gap', label: 'The structural gap' },
  { id: 'what-congress-votes-on', label: 'What Congress votes on' },
  { id: 'net-interest', label: 'Net interest' },
  { id: 'the-laws', label: 'The laws' },
  { id: 'passed-signed', label: 'Who passed it, who signed it' },
  { id: 'where-money-comes-from', label: 'Where the money comes from' },
  { id: 'by-state', label: 'By state' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'What this cannot tell you' },
]
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Government | US federal finance"
  description="Federal taxation, spending, debt and the votes behind them, FY1962 to FY2025."
  sections={sections}
>
  <p class="kicker">Route 3 of 3</p>
  <h1>Government</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">
    What the federal government collects, what it spends, what it owes, and who voted for
    each of it.
  </p>
  <section id="forty-trillion">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 1</span></p>
    <h2>Ten years, twice the debt</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      The US crossed $40 trillion in August 2026. Ten fiscal years ago it was $19.6
      trillion. Measured against the economy, though, the same decade moved the debt from
      105% of GDP to 124%.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      Debt doubled in ten fiscal years, from $19.57T at the close of FY2016 to $40T in
      August 2026, a 2.04x rise.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Debt doubled in ten fiscal years, from $19.57 trillion at the close of FY2016 to $40 trillion in August 2026."
      title="Total public debt outstanding at fiscal year end, 1995 to 2026"
      source={debt._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(debt._meta)}
      xUnit="Fiscal year"
      yUnit="gross debt in nominal $ trillions, or percent of GDP"
      note="The final point is a daily close, not a fiscal year-end value, and carries no share of GDP because FY2026 GDP is not final."
    >
      <DebtChart client:visible rows={debt.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      Doubling is not new. FY2005 to FY2015 ran $7.9 trillion to $18.2 trillion, a 2.3x
      rise, faster than this decade. What changed is the base. That decade added $10.2
      trillion. This one added $20.3 trillion, an average of $2.0 trillion a year.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      CBO projected in May 2023 that the debt would reach $40 trillion in 2028. It arrived
      about two years early.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="by-state">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 11</span></p>
    <h2>By state, and which states give more than they get</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Every state pays federal tax and every state receives federal spending. Comparing the
      two by state is not the balance-of-payments question it sounds like — what follows is
      narrower on both sides, and says so before it says anything else.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      In FY2025, {statesBalance.data.summary.n_get_more} states and DC received more in federal
      award spending, per person, than the IRS collected from them in gross federal tax; {statesBalance.data.summary.n_give_more} paid more than they received.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel={`Federal gross tax collections against federal award spending by state, FY${statesBalance.data.fy_give}. ${statesBalance.data.summary.n_get_more} states and DC receive more than they pay; ${statesBalance.data.summary.n_give_more} pay more than they receive.`}
      title="What each state gives the federal government and gets back, FY2025"
      source={statesBalance._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(statesBalance._meta)}
      xUnit="State or territory"
      yUnit="federal gross tax collections against federal award spending, per person or in total"
      note="Give is gross IRS collections by filer address; get is USASpending award spending by place of performance. This is narrower than a balance of payments on both sides — read the paragraphs below before comparing states."
    >
      <StateGiveGet client:visible data={statesBalance.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      <strong>What this is not.</strong> This is not a balance of payments. The Rockefeller
      Institute's study of that name is the authoritative version, and its series ends at
      FY2022. What is drawn here is federal tax <strong>collected</strong> in a state against
      federal award spending <strong>performed</strong> in it, both for FY2025, and the two are
      not opposite sides of one ledger.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      <strong>Where the money is counted is not where it lands.</strong> The IRS classifies
      collections by the filer's address, so a corporation's tax is booked to its principal
      office and withholding is booked to the employer's location, which for a border employer
      may sit in a different state from the people it withholds from. Federal spending is
      counted where the work is performed, which sends money to the state hosting a
      <strong> military base</strong> rather than the state its personnel came from, and to a
      <strong> federal retiree's</strong> former duty station rather than where they now live.
      <strong> Medicaid</strong> dollars are recorded against the state that passes them through
      to providers.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      <strong>Neither side is complete.</strong> Award spending excludes most direct benefit
      payments, so the "get" side is smaller than what a state actually receives; gross
      collections include penalties and interest and exclude excise taxes collected by Customs
      and the Alcohol and Tobacco Tax and Trade Bureau. Read the ordering, not the arithmetic.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel={`Each state's own tax collections by category as a share of its total, FY${statesTaxMix.data.fy}.`}
      title="State tax collections by category, as a share of each state's own total"
      source={statesTaxMix._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(statesTaxMix._meta)}
      xUnit="Tax category"
      yUnit="percent of that state's own total tax collections"
      note="A category can be genuinely not levied by a state (Alaska has no general sales tax) rather than merely unreported; the two are shown differently, never both as a dash."
    >
      <StateTaxMix client:visible data={statesTaxMix.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      Give (IRS), get (USASpending) and this state tax mix (Census) are three separately
      discovered vintages, not one dataset: the IRS and USASpending figures above are both
      FY2025 by construction, and this Census survey is also its FY2025 vintage — none of the
      three shares a publication schedule, and a future run of this pipeline may find them out
      of step again. Territories (Puerto Rico, Guam, the US Virgin Islands, the Northern
      Mariana Islands and American Samoa) appear in the award-spending "get" figures but have
      no state-level breakdown in IRS Table 5, so they carry a give figure of "no data" rather
      than zero and are not drawn on the map above. Washington DC is drawn on the map and
      counted in the table, but is excluded from the colour range: as the seat of the federal
      government it is an outlier by construction, and including it would compress the scale
      for all 50 states. In the tax-mix table below, "none levied" means the state does not
      collect that tax at all — not that the figure is missing.
    </p>
  </section>

  {sections.slice(1).filter((s) => !built.has(s.id)).map((s) => (
    <section id={s.id}><h2>{s.label}</h2><p class="num">Not built yet.</p></section>
  ))}
</BaseLayout>
